course: dictionary
lesson: 4513
title: Headcount: counting people
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A headcount is a count of people, done by counting heads. Teachers do a headcount on school trips to check that no one is missing. Headcount is a noun, so you can say «do a headcount» or «a headcount of the class».
explain: Companies use the word too, in a more formal way. Here a headcount is the number of people who work in a company. «A company that sacks two hundred people may announce "a headcount reduction", as if it only changed a number on a chart.» The chart shows numbers, but those numbers are real people losing their jobs.
explain: Use the context to choose the meaning. If the sentence is about a trip, a register or a class, it means the people counted there and then. If it is about workers, jobs or business news, it means how many people work for the company. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Headcount means the number of people present, or the number of people working in a company.
fact: To do a headcount means to count people, usually one by one.
fact: Headcount can also be the total number of people in a maths problem.
fact: «Reduce headcount» means to have fewer workers in a company.
fact: Context clues: a trip, a class or a register points to counting people; workers, jobs or a company point to the formal meaning.
exercise: On the school trip the teacher says, «Let's do a headcount before we leave.» What does the teacher do? | Counts the sandwiches in the bags | *Counts the people to check nobody is missing | Asks everyone to sing a song | Counts the buses in the car park
exercise: «The company will reduce its headcount next year.» What does headcount mean here? | *The number of people who work there | The number of coins in the till | The number of trees in the park | The number of times the company has a meeting
exercise: In «A company that sacks two hundred people», what does sacks mean? | Hugs them warmly | *Dismisses, takes away people's jobs | Sews them together | Counts them carefully
test: The bus has a headcount of thirty-one, including the teacher. What does headcount mean here? | The number of bags on the bus | The number of wheels on the bus | *The number of people on the bus | The number of seats on the bus
test: Why would business news say «headcount reduction»? | Everyone at the company has had a pay rise | The company has lost its chart | More people are coming to work there | *Fewer people work at the company
exam: Which meaning fits best in «Please do a headcount before we go in»? | Count the doors in the building | Count the words in a story | *Count the people who are present | Count the marks on a test
